From 751191ad89a0bd7f5416ee6537af1c7315ac6abc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Frederick Rodrigues <fredaudio@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 22:11:23 +1100
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] Fix the VideoToolbox JPEG decoder on Apple Silicon and
 enable it by default

The decoder "crashed on M1 and later" because a packet the hardware decoder
rejects (truncated or corrupt JPEG after a USB hiccup) produced no pixel
buffer, and the frame was still emitted with data == NULL.

VTRgbPacketProcessor now:
- checks every CoreMedia / VideoToolbox status and drops packets that don't
  decode, as TurboJPEG does;
- waits for the output callback before returning (it writes to the stack);
- recreates the session when the system invalidates it (sleep, media engine
  reset) and retries the packet;
- reports good() == false after 30 consecutive failures, and is wrapped in
  RgbDecoderFallback (default and explicit selection), so TurboJPEG takes over
  at run time;
- decodes into IOSurface-backed, Metal-compatible BGRA buffers locked
  read-only, copying only if the rows are padded;
- passes the sample size (CMSampleBufferCreateReady).

On an M3 Ultra a 1080p 4:2:2 frame costs ~0.9 ms of CPU with VideoToolbox
vs 3-12 ms with TurboJPEG. ENABLE_VIDEOTOOLBOX now defaults to ON on all
Macs. New tests (tests/test_vt_rgb_decoder.cpp): parity with TurboJPEG,
corrupt packets, the unhealthy threshold, fallback hand-over and four
decoders in parallel; clean under ASan + UBSan.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
